  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 154 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThursday... \nOnce again, Thursday will continue the trend of calm and seasonable\nsummer days, albeit with some differences. The most notable of \nthese is the combination of the ridge retrograding westward and \na shortwave translating through its northern periphery in the \nGreat Plains. Locally, southerly to southeasterly low-level flow\naround the high pressure to our east will advect in a moister \nairmass. Together, this will set the stage for our next round of\nshowers and storms Thursday night and Friday.\n\nThursday Night through Friday... \nAs previously mentioned, a combination of the upper level ridge\nretrograding and a shortwave trough will lead to weakening in \nthe ridge through the Great Plains into the Great Lakes region. \nAs this shortwave translates through the northeast edge of the \nridge, it will provide lift across the area. This will result in\nthe potential for showers and potentially a few thunderstorms. \n\nWith any chances for thunderstorms comes the question of how likely \nsevere thunderstorms are. Environmental parameters suggest that \nsevere storms have a lower probability (10-20%), as deep layer shear \nof 10-30 kts and MUCAPE of less than 1000 J/kg would provide only a \nmarginal degree of support for any organized convection. However, \nparameters upstream in the Great Plains are better aligned to \nsupport organized convection, suggesting there could be potential \nfor organized storms to form in this area. Additional\nuncertainty is in the timing of this system, as guidance differs\non how long the trough will take to move through the region, One\nexample is the deterministic ECMWF, which has the system linger\ninto the weekend and would thus have a longer period of\nrainfall. We will continue to monitor this system in the coming\ndays.\n\nSaturday through Monday... \nFollowing the passage of the shortwave, upper level flow will \nbecome northerly as an area of high pressure sets up over the \nnorthern Great Lakes. This will result in cooler temperatures as\nwe enter the weekend, with the potential for highs in the upper\n70s to low 80s Saturday. Over the course of the weekend, subtle\nridging and low-level warm advection will bring temperatures up\ninto the 80s once more.",
